<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chitradurga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chitradurga is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known for its hill fortress with seven concentric rings of walls on granite hills. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a> were found nearby. The fort was enlarged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a> of Chitradurga, who ruled from it from the 16th century until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> took it in 1779. Local legend celebrates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onake-obavva">Onake Obavva</a>, who defended a breach in the walls with a pestle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chitradurga.jpg" alt="Chitradurga" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basavarajmin21 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GALIMANTAPA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
